# Attribution

This project began from [TileOPs](https://github.com/tile-ai/TileOPs) by Tile-AI, and still
carries substantial code from it under the MIT License: the operator layer
(`src/tileops/ops/`), the manifest framework (`src/tileops/manifest/`), the performance model
(`src/tileops/perf/`), the tracer (`src/tileops/trace/`), the workload, test, benchmark and
tooling layers (`workloads/`, `tests/`, `benchmarks/`, `scripts/`), and most of `docs/design/`.

`src/tileops/kernels/` is no longer one of them. Upstream's tree there held 158 CUDA/Hopper
kernels; all of it was removed, and the directory now holds the AscendC kernels, which are
ours -- as are the 910B1 hardware profile (`src/tileops/perf/profiles/ascend910b1.yaml`), the
NPU probes under `tests/npu/`, the device-time measurement basis, and the handwritten-baseline
provenance machinery. The parts of upstream's kernel tree that were never about a device --
the call records the op layer validates against, pooling's output-extent arithmetic --
were kept and moved into `src/tileops/ops/`, where the paragraph above still covers them.

## Structures read from tilelang-ascend

`src/tileops/kernels/` is our own code, but two kernels in it follow the *structure* of
examples in [tilelang-ascend](https://github.com/tile-ai/tilelang-ascend) (MIT,
Copyright (c) Tile-AI Corporation), which were read for the idea rather than copied:

* `src/tileops/kernels/gemm.py::_build_gemm_pipelined` and
  `src/tileops/kernels/gemm_epilogue.py` -- the three-stage AscendC matmul pipeline
  (asynchronous GM->L1 stage in front of an L1->L0A/L0B rotation, driven by
  `set_flag`/`wait_flag` event rotation) follows the shape of
  `examples/gemm/example_gemm_intrinsic.py`, and the two-scope Cube/Vector split follows
  `examples/deepseek_v4/int8_gemm.py`. The tile plan, the event numbering, the drain
  accounting and the fallback conditions are ours (T264 / `docs/reports/R264.md`).
* `src/tileops/kernels/gemm.py::_build_bmm_pipelined` and
  `src/tileops/kernels/gemm_splitk.py::_build_splitk_partials` -- both are the same
  three-stage pipeline as `_build_gemm_pipelined` above, extended with a batch index
  and with a K-chunk index respectively. They inherit that entry's attribution; no
  further tilelang-ascend source was read for them (T267 / `docs/reports/R268.md`).

Both copyright notices are retained in [LICENSE](LICENSE), as the MIT License requires.

## T301C GEMM variants

`src/tileops/ops/gemm/variants.py` and `_variants_kernels.py` reuse our own
`src/tileops/kernels/gemm.py` builders and reimplement the Cube accumulation /
separate vector epilogue idea read from
`tilelang-ascend/examples/deepseek_v4/int8_gemm.py` (MIT, Copyright (c)
Tile-AI Corporation). The GEMV example `examples/gemv/example_gemv_v.py` was
read for comparison; the implementation delegates to our existing GEMM kernel.
No example module was imported and no contiguous source passage was copied.
The explicit packed-format decoding and HCCL compositions are new T301C code.

T301 A (2026-09-14): src/tileops/ops/reduction/t301a.py and t301a_kernels.py
reference Tile-AI's in-tree kernels/reduction.py, elementwise_binary.py and
 elementwise_predicate.py, and the MIT / Copyright (c) Tile-AI examples
 tilelang-ascend/examples/sort/example_merge_sort.py and
 tilelang-ascend/examples/unsorted_segment_sum/unsorted_segment_sum.py.
The new scalar merge/scan and vector affine/scatter code is independently
written from those ideas; the existing Welford, Where, Sum and comparison
builders are reused directly. No example module is imported and no example
body is copied. Full original workload blocks and their donors are recorded
in src/tileops/manifest/t301a.yaml and docs/reports/R301A-data/resume/.
T301 A Compare NE additionally reuses kernels/elementwise_unary.py's logical
not and the complement-of-EQ idea documented in
/home/dyq/cannbot/work/custom/count_nonzero/count_nonzero.py (read as text).

T301 B normalization additions (src/tileops/ops/norm/t301*.py)
----------------------------------------------------------------
The new operators use rewritten, fixed-geometry vector kernels. Design sources:
TileOPs src/tileops/kernels/elementwise_unary.py and normalization_spatial.py
(aligned vector loads/stores, fp32 reductions, explicit buffer-reuse barriers);
/home/dyq/cannbot/work/custom/gemma_rms_norm/proto.yaml and gemma_rms_norm.py
(Gemma's fp32 1+weight contract and sqrt/div arithmetic); and normalization
signatures 88, 90, 96, 98, 100-102, 104-108 of /home/dyq/op-list-150.pdf.
No external implementation passages were copied and no example was imported.
The scoped provenance record is src/tileops/ops/norm/NOTICE.T301.
